concepts

A:Data model:
    1:Table:collections of related data(like sql)
    2:Item:is like row each with unique primary key.
    3:Attributes:is like column ,actual data filed

notes here is all column should be necessary have data or null , it can be empty also
so we can say dynamo db is schema less.

    pros:fast/pay what is stored/ migration is easy
    cons:no reliability of correctness of data.

B:dynamo indexing:

1:partition key->uniquely identifier that identify the physical storage(nodes) location
                 through hash(mainly consistent hashing).
2:sort key->(optional which is sort the data within the partition).used for ordering
             and range queries(each sort key we end up having b tree stored in memory
             and used for range queries).
both works together

primary key is combination of both the thing:(partition+sort)key

example :
e-commerce application :p-key(user_id),s_key(order_id)
chatAp application :p_key(chat_id),s_key(message_id)
social media :p_key(user_id),s_key(post_id)

what if we want to sort the data other than sort key so for that we have 2 indexes
secondary indexes:
    1:global secondary indexes(GSI)->different partition key and sort key from the
                                    original table to query by any attributes.
                                    ->eventual consistent
                                    ->Can be created or deleted at any time.
                                    ->No size limits on the index or partitions.
    2:local secondary indexes(LSI):->partition key must be same as base table
                                    ->sort key must different from base table
                                    ->support strong consistency
                                    ->strict 10gb limit

#transaction in dynamo db:
->it support transaction(all the operation happens atomically)

#dynamo db architecture
->scale through consistent hashing through partition key.

#fault tolerance
->writes come to leader and after this leader asynchronously propagates changes
  to replicas across multiple AZs.
->reading from replicas(by default eventual consistent) but can enable
  strong consistency reads.

#Dynamo db advanced feature

1:Dax(dynamo db accelerator):It is in-memory caching layer which provides response
                           in microsecond for read heavy workloads with automatic-read
                           through caching.
                           enable it from AWS.

DDB stream:Real time change tracking of table modification(inserts,updates,deletes)
           to trigger downstream processing like lambda functions,replication or
           analytics.
           use-case :you want updates on both the places,eventdb and elastic search use
                     dynamo db stream(which can be lambda)


